In article <371@clover.warwick.ac.uk> ekg@cs.warwick.ac.uk (Kevan Gelling) writes:
>...tried to save the game... It gave a 'alock.1 not found'
> ...
>This has happened before - but only when I've tried to go back up some
>stairs. Is it a game bug or the system setup (UNIX 4.? BSD).

	This sounds like the behavior of a shared playground with "NETWORK"
not defined.  That is, the playground is being shared by processes on more
than one machine.  If you don't set the option in unixconf.h, when a new
game starts it re-uses a lock letter ("a") if the process number that is
currently using that letter can't be found on the machine.  This breaks
because the process might still be alive but on another machine...

	If this isn't the case, you need to look for some other way in which
a similar effect could result.  (Another of the tests for a "moldy" game
is the age of the lock file.  If you tend to suspend hacks for days instead
of saving them this could result, I think.)  Check out the startup code
(look for the #ifndef NETWORK in the source to find the right area) and
see what you might be breaking there...

